model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A pool app API route on the app origin (summary, series, overview, personal overview and account label) still answers its page \"unavailable\" for any failure it does not name, and the page learns nothing more. Each failure the route's catch-all answers with 500 or above is reported to the observability adapter through the hosted error reporting, tagged with its route and, when the pool named one, the pool's closed refusal code. A report holds only the failure's type and its message as telemetry records it, with the message's URLs, addresses, quoted values and long digit-bearing runs replaced; never the error's fields, cause or stack, request content or account data. The direct sink's privacy projection passes only those closed fields. A failed pool command keeps the pool's status and a listed `error` code beside its tagged error, never the pool's message, so the error's wire encoding is unchanged. Nothing below 500 reports, nor do the pool's own named 400, 401, 403, 404 and 413 refusals, which the page still reads as unavailable; any other pool answer the route turns into its 503 reports. A failure marked to be ignored never reports, and a failure whose fields cannot be read reports only as an unrecognized failure. A defect the catch-all does not reach is reported by the Worker without a route tag. Every 5xx, named or unnamed, reports on each occurrence, except the deliberate `history_too_large` refusal: it reports once per pool within an hour in each isolate, because every load of that summary returns it until a later rollup lands."
    }
  }
}
